Tracing the seed-0 run showed speaker assignment was already right (the guest's 0.46 s echo is absorbed by min_turn); the fragments came from pauses of 0.76-0.86 s, just over the old 0.6 s merge_gap, around pieces shorter than the old 1.0 s min_duration. So the fix is in _merge_short's thresholds rather than in diarization handling. Merging across another speaker's interruption (the issue's SEGMENT_MAX_INTERRUPTION idea) was rejected: the merged line would span the guest's line in time, and fit/mix assume segments are sequential and non-overlapping; a real reply (the unseeded run's 0.92 s いいところ, well supported by diarization) is kept as its own line instead. The 0.1 s turn filter is a cheap guard against millisecond turns (a 34 ms one at 35.35 s) tipping a word's speaker; it falls back to all turns if every turn is that short. Tests use the real piece spans because the result depends on them: evenly spaced characters made a 0.22 s 思う with a 1.13 s pause, which correctly does not merge.
